#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231291 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231418 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "742531ae-be2d-5fc8-8ef8-4f745c93bacd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "b7a81b9e-d12c-55c7-b755-8f4e5b8115c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-21859" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'-6y = f(x), \\qquad y(1)=4\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_p$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_p$ satisfies $y_p(1)=0.$*\n\n![](images/d9/d95dc53c303ddf919518e2270cfe6f4314712b414a65f54fb7d228783fa0e214.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form $y' + P(x)y = f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of $y' - 6y = 0$ is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator $y' - 6y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x) \\sim Ae^{6x} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (a_n \\cos(n\\pi x) + b_n \\sin(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=4.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =A{e}^{6(1)}+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 4 & =A{e}^{6}+0 \\\\ A & =4{e}^{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\ny_c = (4e^{-6})e^{6x} = 4e^{6x-6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) \\sim \n\\bbox[4pt,border: 0.5px solid black]{4e^{6x-6}} +\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (a_n \\cos(n\\pi x) + b_n \\sin(n\\pi x)).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231293 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231419 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81f5380b-7a7d-52c5-a530-3768e7cf3143" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "97290d74-457b-522d-991d-7891e5a01d28" :question/math-academy-id "e-21876" :question/problem "$$\ny''+ 7\\pi^2 y = f(x), \\qquad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n![](images/9f/9f77e2dc9619cf5cb270cd68efaf2432a8dbf738e06b33dcdcc18602b5032b1b.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form $y'' + \\omega^2 y = f(x)$ is given by $y = y_c + y_p,$ where $y_c$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution $y_p.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \n\\dfrac{16(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation $\\lambda^2 + 7\\pi^2 = 0$ gives roots $\\lambda = \\pm \\sqrt{7}\\pi \\textrm i.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x) + B\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $y_p(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition $y'(0)=0.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\ny'(x) \\sim B(\\sqrt{7}\\pi)\\cos(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x) + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \n\\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{n\\pi^2\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nApplying $y'(0)=0$ and noting that $\\cos(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =B(\\sqrt{7}\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(28-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B(\\sqrt{7}\\pi ) & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(28-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(28-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{\\sqrt{7}n{\\pi }^{3}(28-{n}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into $y_c = B\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim \n\\bbox[4pt,border: 0.5px solid black]{-\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x)\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{\\sqrt{7}n\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)}}\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \n\\dfrac{16(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231418 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "742531ae-be2d-5fc8-8ef8-4f745c93bacd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "6f6ad64c-d62f-565f-ba15-8bbc501a2f16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230282}] :question/id #uuid "5c1a49a3-d524-53b2-a5dc-a7db56f9b9c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343430" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n$y^{′} + 5y = f(x), y(0) = 2$\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_{p}$ satisfies $y_{p}(0) = 0$.*\n\nThe solution is\n\n$y(x)∼$\n{{field-1}}\n$+ ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(a_{n}\\cos (nπx) + b_{n}\\sin (nπx))$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231418 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "742531ae-be2d-5fc8-8ef8-4f745c93bacd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "783a4a68-b90a-52b7-85fe-b70668c382af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230283}] :question/id #uuid "c997056a-b985-5f20-bdf9-319140e52042" :question/math-academy-id "q-343433" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n$y^{′} + 3y = f(x), y(1) = 5$\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_{p}$ satisfies $y_{p}(1) = 0$.*\n\nThe solution is\n\n$y(x)∼$\n{{field-1}}\n$+ ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(a_{n}\\cos (nπx) + b_{n}\\sin (nπx))$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231419 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81f5380b-7a7d-52c5-a530-3768e7cf3143" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce2f1c7b-a4fc-5268-91ca-e63ea6ba485f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (\\sqrt{5}x)(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{nb_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230286} {:answer/id #uuid "d98cb78c-8304-501e-8b76-145d03bc45dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (\\sqrt{5}x)(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{nb_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230287} {:answer/id #uuid "149cfff1-6f68-5b1c-816b-22b353455f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (\\sqrt{5}x)(4 - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)nb_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230288} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3a4ebb-2118-5a42-b1e7-432cc4416187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (\\sqrt{5}x)(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{nb_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230289}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fd04368a-dac0-5433-8c72-d4cb4522f0aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230285}] :question/id #uuid "77e4924e-4e5a-5efe-907f-1d410dafb965" :question/math-academy-id "q-343467" :question/problem "$y^{″} + 5y = f(x), y(0) = 0, y^{′}(0) = 4$\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $2π$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.$\\cos (\\sqrt{5}x)(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{nb_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}})$ {{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231419 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81f5380b-7a7d-52c5-a530-3768e7cf3143" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "921bdc7f-b937-5c85-8d1c-45160b4898b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (\\sqrt{3}x)(∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n} - 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230291} {:answer/id #uuid "e64108fe-60fc-5d45-85cb-bf30bf8c4504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (\\sqrt{3}x)(5 - ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230292} {:answer/id #uuid "f71fe8d5-4628-56c8-880f-a23596711584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (\\sqrt{3}x)(5 - ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230293} {:answer/id #uuid "6b541c09-24af-57f3-a3c6-0c733f830a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (\\sqrt{3}x)(5 + ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230294}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3834b1f8-740b-5544-92ee-3291442f589d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230290}] :question/id #uuid "30713f82-3138-52e4-9692-3d6512a210a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-343471" :question/problem "$y^{″} + 3y = f(x), y(0) = 5, y^{′}(0) = 0$\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an even, $2π$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.$\\cos (\\sqrt{3}x)(5 - ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n})$ {{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231418 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "742531ae-be2d-5fc8-8ef8-4f745c93bacd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97309906-dff0-599d-b092-0740a0732b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97309906-dff0-599d-b092-0740a0732b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365944} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5699544-5216-55d0-b396-9487609e9ee4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "593a3349-a7fc-5c8f-8d89-90616a43f4a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343757" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=f(x),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $3$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+4y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+4y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=1.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{-4(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 1 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{e}^{-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231418 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "742531ae-be2d-5fc8-8ef8-4f745c93bacd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21b93f35-5863-505d-95aa-0b03a081a02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{5x-15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21b93f35-5863-505d-95aa-0b03a081a02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{5x-15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365947} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f550799-2c78-5c0d-95ef-a64651fc88f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a5b1020-667d-5b46-950b-9e388e936e0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-343942" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=f(x),\\,y(3)=-2\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(3)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}-5y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}-5y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(3)=-2.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(3) & =A{e}^{5(3)}+{y}_{p}(3) \\\\ -2 & =A{e}^{15}+0 \\\\ A & =-2{e}^{-15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(-2{e}^{-15}){e}^{5x}=-2{e}^{5x-15}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-2{e}^{5x-15}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231419 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81f5380b-7a7d-52c5-a530-3768e7cf3143" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7a87f86-90b0-5b7d-9095-19d6fbf68ef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365950} {:answer/id #uuid "59abbacc-a27b-5a19-ac83-400c63f14c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365951} {:answer/id #uuid "6f06faa6-96dc-5bf0-bb0f-d24428537c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{2\\sqrt{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365952} {:answer/id #uuid "7f76102e-5080-58eb-93bc-ef21e4f0b0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)(-5-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{b}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f06faa6-96dc-5bf0-bb0f-d24428537c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{2\\sqrt{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365952} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b5e0295-3d2a-5ea3-b1a9-dd01a18b1b65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e72e085b-8209-5b57-8832-a5f8ba9155db" :question/math-academy-id "q-343980" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-5\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $4\\pi $-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n}{2}x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n}{2}x).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+3=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-5.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{2}{b}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n}{2}x)\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=-5$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =B\\sqrt{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{2}{b}_{n} \\\\ B\\sqrt{3} & =-5-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{2}{b}_{n} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{2\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{2\\sqrt{3}})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n}{2}x)\n$$"}]}
